/grade
/fakevendor
*.test
.DS_Store
testdata/slowtool/
testdata/mutants/

# Compiled binaries.
#
# `go build` names its output after the directory, so a build inside ch05/
# leaves ch05/ch05, and `go build -o bin` leaves ch05/bin. The exercise SOURCE
# under ch0* IS tracked -- the grader compiles and drives it, and it is the
# only thing that proves the public API is usable from outside the module.
# Only the build output belongs here.
#
# This was previously spelled `/ch0`, which matched NOTHING: gitignore does
# not prefix-match without a wildcard, so `/ch0` matches only a root entry
# named exactly "ch0". Three 9MB binaries were committed while that rule
# looked like it was working.
#
# These patterns are deliberately depth-independent. From Chapter 5 on, a
# submission is repo-root-shaped (agent/ + chNN/), so a snapshot under
# solutions/ nests a SECOND copy of the whole layout: solutions/ch07/ch05/bin
# is a real path. Depth-anchored spellings like `solutions/*/bin` cannot keep
# up with that, and two binaries were staged before this was fixed. A pattern
# with no slash matches at any depth; `**/` means the same thing explicitly.
#
# The graders BUILD these binaries into the submission tree every time they
# run, so deleting them by hand does not keep them gone. Only the ignore rule
# does.
bin
**/ch0*/ch0*
# ...but the same shape names SOURCE as well as binaries: the chapter
# exercises live in directories like solutions/ch06/ch06/ (section 6.10's
# author/editor/reviewer pipeline, with its own go.mod). The rule above
# swallowed those whole, so a fresh clone had no exercise and Chapter 6
# scored 45/100. A trailing slash matches directories only, so this
# re-includes the exercise source while the built binaries -- which are
# files, not directories -- stay ignored.
!**/ch0*/ch0*/

# Agent working directories: tool output and scratch, created by any manual
# run. Run OUTPUT, not source.
cr/

# Event logs are run OUTPUT, not source. Tracking one meant every manual run of
# a solution showed up as a modification and added noise to every git status.
# The rule covers *.log rather than one filename because the default log name is
# derived from the executable: a binary built from solutions/ch03 writes
# ch03.log, and one you named yourself writes whatever you named it.
*.log

# Built binaries and runtime state. These were previously anchored to /agent/
# because solutions/chNN/ tracked files with the same names, and an unanchored
# pattern would have shadowed them. That is no longer true: 72MB of committed
# binaries -- solutions/ch05, ch09-ch13 and ch17 -- were purged from history on
# 2026-09-27, which is what makes the depth-independent spelling safe now.
#
# Depth-independence matters here for the same reason it does for `bin` above:
# a snapshot under solutions/ nests a second copy of the whole repo layout, so
# the binary can appear at solutions/ch17/ensemble or deeper, and a rule spelled
# solutions/*/ensemble cannot keep up.
#
# CAREFUL: `ensemble` and `virtual-user` name DIRECTORIES as well as binaries --
# agent/skills/ensemble/SKILL.md holds the primary skill, and agent/cmd/
# virtual-user/ is source. A bare slashless pattern would ignore those whole,
# silently untracking the primary skill. Trailing slash matches directories
# only, so the re-includes below keep the source while the built files -- which
# are files, not directories -- stay ignored. Same idiom as `!**/ch0*/ch0*/`.
**/ensemble
!**/ensemble/
**/virtual-user
!**/virtual-user/

# Always files, never directories, so no re-include is needed.
settings.json
virtual-user.jsonl

# dlv drops these next to the package it builds. Slashless so it matches
# at any depth, for the same reason as `bin` above.
__debug_bin*

# Python bytecode from helper scripts
__pycache__/
.nightly/

# Agent runtime artifacts.
#
# Running a grader against a solution snapshot executes the agent with that
# snapshot as its working directory, so it writes these files INTO the tree
# you are about to commit. The ch19 snapshot picked up request.json and
# prior_request.json exactly that way: grade first, `git add` second, and the
# cache lens output ships as part of the solution.
save.json
save.json.bak
save.json.journal
request.json
prior_request.json
